const SkillMD = "---\nname: ship-it\ndescription: Use the repository shipping contract for SessionStart and Stop hooks. Initializes once at the beginning of the local workday for a repository/worktree, then ships verified changes at the actual end of work and hands the revision to deploy-it without project-specific Git gates.\n---\n\nThe SessionStart hook runs `ship-it start` once at the beginning of the local workday for each repository/worktree. Agents do not run `ship-it start`. At the actual end of work, the Stop hook invokes `ship-it` with no arguments after the agent has implemented, verified, and reviewed the change; it stages every file, bypasses Git hooks and signing, creates an automatic commit and calendar tag, merges directly into the remote-default branch, and pushes. Agents do not run `ship-it`, poll the hook, or retry it. Do not replace this flow with raw Git commands, a pull request, or project-specific logic in `ship.sh`.\n\nTreat the hooks as the delivery path for each repository/worktree. Build, test, and review prepare the work; the Stop hook resolves the repository and performs its automatic `deploy-it` handoff. The agent finishes after its implementation, bounded verification, and review, without adding a shipping command or checkpoint loop. Do not run a separate commit, push, or deployment path around the hooks. If the deployment handoff fails after Git shipping, report the shipped revision and exact deployment failure; do not retry automatically or describe the cycle as deployed.\n\nAt the start of the work, inspect the selected Git remote and tracked repository content for `git.nixc.us` or `woodpecker.nixc.us`. Activate migration work only when either host appears or the user explicitly requests the migration. When activated, steer Git source to an exact private GitHub owner and repository, OCI images to exact private `ghcr.io` package targets, and Woodpecker pipelines to GitHub Actions on the self-hosted local runner. Confirm those external targets and the visible production acceptance result before making external changes. Preserve Git history, branches, and tags; keep repositories and packages private unless the user explicitly authorizes public access; copy every required OCI platform and immutable tag or digest; update repository and deployment references; and verify access plus production health before retiring a legacy endpoint. Do not delete a legacy repository or image automatically.\n\nUse the local runner labels and operating model documented in `~/dev/gh-runner`; never switch a stalled or insufficient job to GitHub-hosted runners. Diagnose the local runner first: compare the checked-in scripts and launchd plists with their installed runtime copies, inspect launchd, tmux, runner containers, and logs, then fix and test `~/dev/gh-runner`. Treat GitHub-hosted runner labels as a shipping defect. Use development subagents only when the migration has independent discovery, rewrite, or verification workstreams that materially benefit from them.\n\nAt final shipping time, hard-fail before repository mutation unless every ordinary job in each tracked or unignored `.github/workflows/*.yml` or `.yaml` file uses a label profile documented in `~/dev/gh-runner`: `[self-hosted, Linux, ARM64, leopere, local]` (optionally `docker` or `public-safe`) or `[self-hosted, macOS, ARM64, leopere, local, native]`. A job may instead call an existing local reusable workflow with `$/` or `./`; check that workflow under the same rule. Block remote, dynamic, missing, and unserviceable runner selections. Recheck the final merged tree before a no-op handoff or push. There is no billing-based fallback, public-runner fallback, or hosted-runner exception.\n\nReview the complete worktree before finishing. The Stop hook ships the verified, production-safe work at the actual end of the session or workday. Prompt completion, urgent checkpoints, and intermediate turns do not initiate shipping. Each hook run includes every repository change; do not leave files uncommitted or create a partial shipment. If any worktree change is known to break the remote-default branch or production, fix it before finishing.\n\nAfter every successful shipping resolution, `ship-it` must invoke the installed `deploy-it` binary with the immutable commit, remote, branch, and tag, including when the revision was already shipped. A repository without a `.deploy-it.json` in that shipped commit is explicitly non-deployable and deploy-it skips it. For a deployable repository, the shipped tracked contract is the authority; deploy-it does not require a machine-local trust record. A deployment failure occurs after Git shipping, must return nonzero, and must never trigger an automatic retry or rollback.\n\nIf `ship-it` reports a merge conflict, resolve every conflicted file according to the user's intent and rerun `ship-it`; otherwise do not ask permission to ship. Let ship-it create or repair the root `ship.sh`; it preserves old custom behavior separately, which may be simplified into an explicitly named development or deployment command when relevant. Never point deploy-it back at `ship.sh` or `ship-it`.\n"

